\subsection{Results of Hybrid Evasion Attacks}

%\partitle{Experimental Settings} 
We also explore combining evasion attack methods from different phases to enhance overall attack effectiveness. Specifically, we hereby evaluate three types of hybrid evasion attacks: \textbf{(1) HA1:} AutoEncoder, Adversarial Training, and Reprogramming. \textbf{(2) HA2:} WaveletFilter, DP-SGD, and  Randomized Smoothing. \textbf{(3) HA3:} WaveletFilter and Reprogramming. The results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:hybrid}.

\begin{takeawaybox}
    \textbf{Takeaway 4:} Combining attacks from different phases can significantly enhance the attack effectiveness.
\end{takeawaybox}

Our empirical results corroborate this takeaway, demonstrating the heightened threat posed by hybrid evasion attacks. For instance, HA1 and HA3 successfully circumvent nearly all evaluated auditing methods, substantially degrading the performance metrics and causing audit failures. HA2 also demonstrates considerable efficacy, evading all the auditing methods, but also degrades the utility of the trained models. This finding highlights the feasibility of a sophisticated adversary to combine attacks to achieve better attack effectiveness.

