\tightpartitle{Experimental Settings} We conduct evaluations on the (100-class subset of) ImageNet dataset~\cite{deng2009imagenet} using a different adversary's model architecture, MobileViT~\cite{mehta2021mobilevit}, trained from scratch. 
%This setup introduces greater dataset complexity (higher resolution, variance) and architectural diversity compared to our main CIFAR-10/ResNet-18 experiments. Crucially, 
We maintain the realistic assumption that if an auditor needed to train auxiliary models for their auditing method (\eg, shadow models for MIA), they would use a standard architecture (ResNet-18), thus creating an architectural mismatch with the ViT suspect model. %The same suite of evasion attacks from \sys was applied in this setting.

\tightpartitle{Results} Consistent with our primary findings, the results in Table~\ref{tab:imagenet} demonstrate that existing dataset auditing methods remain highly vulnerable to evasion attacks in this more challenging setting. Both IF-based and EF-based auditing techniques frequently failed to correctly identify the usage of the ImageNet victim dataset when attacks like AutoEncoder-based denoising, adversarial training, or Reprogramming were employed. The architectural mismatch (transformer-based model and auditor's CNN) and the increased dataset complexity do not confer robustness to the evaluated auditing methods.

