\tightpartitle{Experimental Settings} Beyond training models from scratch, fine-tuning pre-trained models on a task-specific dataset is a common and efficient paradigm in modern DL. We simulate the fine-tuning scenario using a ResNet-18 pre-trained on ImageNet-1000 and then fine-tune it using CIFAR-10 as the target (victim) dataset. Other experimental settings remain consistent with our main experiments in Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}.

\tightpartitle{Results}
The outcomes of applying evasion attacks in the fine-tuning scenario are presented in Table~\ref{tab:finetuning}. The results indicate that dataset auditing methods exhibit vulnerabilities similar to those observed when training from scratch. Both IF-based methods and EF-based methods frequently fail when confronted with evasion attacks such as DP-SGD, RS, or Reprogramming. 
%Even though fine-tuning modifies fewer parameters compared to training from scratch, the process still imparts characteristics or preserves implanted features that the auditing methods attempt to detect, and these signals remain susceptible to adversarial manipulation. 
This suggests that the inherent weaknesses of current auditing techniques are not limited to models trained from scratch but also extend to the fine-tuning paradigm. %, further emphasizing the need for more resilient auditing.

\subsection{Forgery Attacks in the Black-box Scenario}
\label{apd:blackbox}

This section evaluates forgery attacks when the adversary only possesses black-box access to the target model. Lacking parameter access hinders gradient-based attacks. Thus, the adversary may distill a substitute model from the target model using an auxiliary dataset. Then they can conduct forgery attacks on this substitute model, relying on the transferability of adversarial attacks to affect the original target model.

\vspace{0.4em}
\tightpartitle{Experimental Settings} We simulate the black-box setting where the target model is the ResNet-18 and the substitute model employs the MobileNetV2 architecture\cite{sandler2018mobilenetv2}. All other settings remain consistent with the main experiments.

\vspace{0.4em}
\tightpartitle{Results} From Table~\ref{tab:forgery-blackbox}, the success rate of forgery attacks generally decreases in the black-box setting compared to the white-box scenario. However, despite the reduction, most dataset auditing methods (\eg, DUA, DVBW, and UBW) remain susceptible to forgery even under these stricter black-box constraints. This indicates that even without direct model access, achieving dataset forgery remains a practical threat against a significant portion of current auditing techniques.

