Theorem \ref{Annulus Twist Theorem} has a pleasing interpretation we call the ``Coiler's Conundrum''. As in Figure \ref{fig: coilerscord}, a lazy householder takes a tangled extension cord, wraps it into a coil and plugs the ends into themselves. If the resulting knot is the unknot, would it still be the unknot if one end were first wrapped through the coil $q \neq 0$ times? Theorem \ref{Annulus Twist Theorem} shows that if $|q| \geq 2$ and the original cord was sufficiently tangled, the resulting knot will not be the unknotted. Figure \ref{Fig:Annulus Twist} shows that if $q = \pm 1$, it is possible to obtain the unknot.

