\title{Compositional Control-Driven Boolean Circuits}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\hypersetup{
  urlcolor=Blue4,
  citecolor=Blue4,
  linkcolor=Blue4
}

\begin{frontmatter}
\author[]{Damian Arellanes \\ Lancaster University, United Kingdom}

\begin{abstract}
Boolean circuits abstract away from physical details to focus on the logical structure and computational behaviour of digital components. Despite they have been studied for many decades, compositionality has been widely ignored or examined in an informal manner, which is a property for combining circuits without delving into their internal structure, while supporting modularity and formal reasoning. In this paper, we address this longstanding theoretical gap by proposing colimit-based operators for compositional circuit construction. We define separate operators for forming sequential, parallel, branchial and iterative circuits. As composites encapsulate explicit control flow, a new model of computation emerges which we refer to as (families of) \emph{control-driven Boolean circuits}. We show how this model is at least as powerful as its classical counterpart. In other words, it is able to non-uniformly compute any Boolean function on inputs of arbitrary length. 
\end{abstract}
\end{frontmatter} keywords: Boolean circuits, algebraic composition, computons, explicit control flow, models of high-level computation

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Introduction}

Families of Boolean circuits define a non-uniform model of computation in the sense a collection of circuits can be used to solve a particular problem (e.g., deciding a formal language) \cite{clote_boolean_2002}. To realise any Boolean function, a functionally complete basis of logical connectives is required for circuit construction such as $\{\land,\lor,\lnot\}$, $\{\implies,\lnot\}$ or $\{\uparrow\}$ \cite{wegener_complexity_1987}. 

Although this model of computation extends beyond Turing machines, because it can be used to solve even undecidable problems, it does not provide theoretical underpinnings to enable the compositional construction of Boolean circuits via well-defined algebraic operators. By composition, we mean forming large complex circuits from simpler ones, without delving into the internals of the composed entities \cite{ghica_complete_2025}. Endowing circuits with compositional capabilities can enhance formal analysis and can allow treating circuits as modular, self-similar black-boxes that can be reused across a wide range of application domains \cite{giannakopoulou_compositional_2018}.

For formal analysis, compositional verification is perhaps the most prominent consequent technique, which allows proving that a property $\phi$ holds for a composite $C$ whenever $\phi$ holds for all the constituents and $\phi$ is preserved across all the composition levels underlying $C$ \cite{dardik_recomposition_2024}. 

Although composition can be realised in many different ways, in this paper we follow up on our idea that every piece of (high- or low-level) computation exhibits either implicit or explicit control flow, no matter how data is exchanged \cite{arellanes_compositional_2024,arellanes_models_2025}. Accordingly, we propose a theory for compositionally constructing Boolean circuits through colimit-based composition operators that define explicit control flow for the computation of Boolean functions in some specific order. We formalise distinct operators for forming sequential, parallel, branchial and iterative circuits. Sequencing and parallelising are obvious in terms of behaviour. While branching captures random choice, iteration allows the repeated computation of some circuit. For sequencing, parallelising and branching we show relevant algebraic properties in terms of identity, commutativity and associativity. We also show how every Boolean function can be computed by the new model we introduce, which we refer to as (families of) \emph{control-driven Boolean circuits}. 

The rest of the paper is structured as follows. Section \ref{sec:model} introduces the formal definition of the new class of circuits we propose and describes colimit-based operations for their compositional construction. This section also presents operational semantics for circuit execution. Section \ref{sec:equivalence} discusses the relationship between control-driven circuits and their classical counterpart in terms of what can be computed. Section \ref{sec:related-work} presents and analyses related work. Section \ref{sec:conclusions} concludes the paper by outlining the final remarks and discussing future directions.

\section{Control-Driven Boolean Circuits}
\label{sec:model}

The computon model \cite{arellanes_compositional_2024} provides a general framework to specify and compose high-level, control-driven computations which dictate the explicit order in which (low- or other high-level) computations must occur. Intuitively, it allows forming graph-like structures (called computons) in which nodes are either computation units or typed ports, while edges are information flows running from units to ports (or vice versa), never between elements of the same kind. A computation unit encapsulates a low-level computation, whereas a port is just a buffer for control signals or other typed data. 

In this paper, we move from the abstract to the concrete by studying a particular class of computons in which computation units are functions that establish an indexed family of NAND operators, while ports are variables holding either control or Boolean values (see Definition \ref{def:cunit}). Accordingly, the set of types on which this class operates consists of two elements: the unit type $\mathbbm{1}$ and the Boolean type $\mathbb{B}$. As high-level computations define control flow for the evaluation of NAND operators in some well-defined order, we refer to this class of computons to as control-driven Boolean circuits (or circuits for short). 

\begin{definition}[Computation Unit]\label{def:cunit}
A computation unit is the indexed family ${(\uparrow_k)_{k\in \mathbb{N}}}$ where ${\uparrow_0=1}$ is a constant function and, for ${k>0}$, ${\uparrow_k}$ is the $k$-ary NAND operator ${\mathbb{B}^k\rightarrow\mathbb{B}}$ given by ${\uparrow_k(b_1,\ldots,b_k)=\lnot(b_1\land\cdots\land b_k)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Control-Driven Boolean Circuit] \label{def:computon}
A control-driven Boolean circuit $\lambda$ is a decuple ${(P,U,I,O,\Sigma,s,t,\sigma,\tau,c)}$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $P$ is a (non-empty) finite set of variables,
\item $U$ is a (possibly empty) finite set of computation units,
\item $I$ is a (possibly empty) finite set of input flows linking variables to computation units,
\item $O$ is a (possibly empty) finite set of output flows linking computation units to variables,
\item ${\Sigma}\subseteq\{\mathbbm{1},\mathbb{B}\}$ is a (non-empty) finite set of types,
\item ${s\colon I\rightarrow P}$ is a function that specifies the source variable of each input flow,
\item ${\tau\colon I\twoheadrightarrow U}$ is a surjective function that specifies the target computation unit of each input flow,
\item ${\sigma\colon O\twoheadrightarrow U}$ is a surjective function that specifies the source computation unit of each output flow,
\item ${t\colon O\rightarrow P}$ is a function that specifies the target variable of each output flow and
\item ${c\colon P\twoheadrightarrow\Sigma}$ is a function that assigns a type to each variable.
\end{itemize}
Such that there is:
\begin{itemize}
\item at least one variable ${p \in P\setminus s(I)}$ with ${c(p)=\mathbbm{1}}$ and
\item at least one variable ${q \in P\setminus t(O)}$ with ${c(q)=\mathbbm{1}}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{notation}\label{not:port-types}
Given a circuit $\lambda$, we call ${p\in P}$ a control variable if it satisfies ${c(p)=\mathbbm{1}}$. Otherwise, if ${c(p)=\mathbb{B}}$, we call it a Boolean variable.
\end{notation}

\begin{notation}\label{not:flows}
For any circuit $\lambda$, we write ${p\xrightarrow{i}u}$ to express ${s(i)=p}$ and ${\tau(i)=u}$ for some ${i\in I}$, some ${p\in P}$ and some ${u\in U}$. Whenever ${p_1\xrightarrow{i_1}u_1\xrightarrow{o_1}p_2\xrightarrow{i_2}\cdots\xrightarrow{i_{n-1}}u_{n-1}\xrightarrow{o_{n-1}}p_n}$ exists, the statement ${p_1\xrightarrow{\exists}p_n}$ holds.
\end{notation}

\begin{notation}\label{not:pre-post-sets}
If ${u \in U}$ is a computation unit of a circuit $\lambda$, the set ${s(\tau^{-1}(u))}$ of variables connected to $u$ and the set ${t(\sigma^{-1}(u))}$ of variables connected from $u$ are denoted ${\bullet u}$ and ${u\bullet}$, respectively. Similarly, for any variable ${p\in P}$, the sets ${\tau(s^{-1}(p))}$ and ${\sigma(t^{-1}(p))}$ of computation units are denoted ${p\bullet}$ and ${\bullet p}$, respectively.
\end{notation}

Definition \ref{def:computon} can be elucidated by expressing a circuit $\lambda$ in the form of the set-valued diagram shown in Figure \ref{fig:computon-diagram} which clearly captures the structure and relation between the components of $\lambda$. From now on, we use natural numbers as subindices to distinguish between different circuits and their components. Evidently, if a symbol for a circuit has no subindex, its components have no subindex either.

\begin{figure}[!h]
\centering
\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&]
 \& O \arrow[dl,twoheadrightarrow,"\sigma"']\arrow[dr, "t"] \& \&  \\
U \& \& P \arrow[r,twoheadrightarrow,"c"] \& \Sigma \\
 \& I \arrow[ul,twoheadrightarrow,"\tau"]\arrow[ur, "s"'] \& \& \\
\end{tikzcd}
\caption{A circuit $\lambda$ as a set-valued diagram.}
\label{fig:computon-diagram}
\end{figure}

A glance at Figure \ref{fig:computon-diagram} reveals that any circuit $\lambda$ has neither dangling computation units nor dangling flows, as a result of imposing surjectivity on $\sigma$ and $\tau$ as well as totality on $s$ and $t$. Notice in Definition \ref{def:computon} that, although it is possible for $\lambda$ to have no computation units and no flows at all, the presence of typed variables is mandatory. In fact, the two restrictions from Definition \ref{def:computon} explicitly state that there must be at least one variable with no incoming flows and at least one variable with no outgoing flows mapped to the unit type $\mathbbm{1}$. Implicitly, this means $\Sigma$ is either ${\{\mathbbm{1}\}}$ or ${\{\mathbbm{1},\mathbb{B}\}}$, i.e., the unit type is always a member of $\Sigma$.

Variables with no incoming flows are called invars whereas variables with no outgoing flows are called outvars. Together, they form the interface of a circuit towards the external world, as captured by Definition \ref{def:interface}. As $\mathbbm{1}$ is the type that represents control signals, circuits always have control invars and control outvars. $\mathbb{B}$-typings are reserved for Boolean values ($0$ or $1$ for convenience). 

\begin{definition}[Circuit Interface]\label{def:interface}
The interface of a circuit $\lambda$ is a tuple ${(P^+,P^-)}$ where ${P^+}$ is the set ${P\setminus t(O)}$ of invars and ${P^-}$ is the set ${P\setminus s(I)}$ of outvars.
\end{definition}

When a circuit exhibits a sequence of flows that take each invar to some outvar, we say soundness is satisfied. This property is formalised in Definition \ref{def:sound}.

\begin{definition}[Sound Circuit]\label{def:sound}
A circuit is sound if every variable ${p\in s(I)\cup P^+}$ satisfies ${p\xrightarrow{\exists}q}$ for some outvar ${q\in P^-}$.
\end{definition}

More technically, Definition \ref{def:sound} applies not only to invars but also to other variables with outgoing flows. We consider these two cases because $P^+\subseteq s(I)$ does not hold in general by the fact $P^+$ and $P^-$ are not necessarily disjoint. A variable $p\in P^+\cap P^-$ is called inoutvar and, according to Definition \ref{def:interface}, it has neither incoming nor outgoing flows. Thus, a circuit with at least one inoutvar does not satisfy soundness.

\emph{Trivial Circuits} are a class of circuits that possess all variables in ${P^+\cap P^-}$. Although they are not sound, these circuits are needed for the coherence of our theory. Figure \ref{fig:computon-trivial} shows that, apart from having all inoutvars, trivial circuits have no computation units and no flows at all.

