\textbf{Embedding Layer:} The embedding layer serves as the first point of contact with the input time series data, mapping each observation to a high-dimensional representational token. This is achieved through a multi-layer perceptron, which embeds the entire series and effectively captures the essence of each feature within the series. The embedding process is mathematically represented as follows:

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{Z}^0_{:, f} = \phi(\mathbf{W}_e \mathcal{O}_{:, f} + \mathbf{b}_e)
\end{equation}
where \(\mathcal{O}_{:, f} \in \mathbb{R}^{T}\) denotes all observation points for feature \( f \) across \( T \) time steps, and \(\mathbf{Z}^0_{:, f} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}\) represents the initial embedded state after applying the embedding layer. Here, \(\mathbf{W}_e \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times T}\) and \(\mathbf{b}_e \in \mathbb{R}^{d}\) are the weight matrix and bias vector of the embedding MLP, respectively, and \(\phi(\cdot)\) denotes a non-linear activation function, such as ReLU.

\textbf{TrmBlock:} The TrmBlock is an integral component that combines self-attention with feed-forward mechanisms and layer normalization, capturing extensive temporal dependencies and addressing non-stationarity and measurement inconsistencies. Each TrmBlock processes the input through several stages:

1. \textbf{Multi-Scaled Attention:}  The multi-scaled attention mechanism enhances the model's ability to capture intricate temporal patterns by processing the $K$ and $V$ matrices through one-dimensional convolutional layers at different stages. This approach allows the model to discern complex temporal patterns across a broad spectrum of scales. Each input token \(\mathbf{Z}\) is embedded to obtain the query (\(\mathbf{Q}\)), $K$ and $V$ matrices, followed by spatial reduction of \(K\) and \(V\) through one dimension depth-wise convolutional layers:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbf{Q} = \mathbf{Z}^i \mathbf{W}_Q, \quad \mathbf{K} = \mathbf{Z}^i \mathbf{W}_K, \quad \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{Z}^i \mathbf{W}_V \\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbf{K}_i = \text{Conv1D}(\mathbf{K}, R_i), \quad \mathbf{V}_i = \text{Conv1D}(\mathbf{V}, R_i) \\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}_i, \mathbf{V}_i) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q} \mathbf{K}_i^\top}{\sqrt{d_{k_i}}}\right) \mathbf{V}_i \\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathbf{Z}^{i+1}_{\text{attn}} = \text{LayerNorm}(\mathbf{Z}^i + \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}_i, \mathbf{V}_i))
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where \(\mathbf{W}_Q, \mathbf{W}_K, \mathbf{W}_V \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}\) are the weight matrices for the query, key, and value projections, respectively. The matrices \(\mathbf{K}_i\) and \(\mathbf{V}_i\) are the convoluted key and value matrices, The scaling ratios \(R_i\) are calibrated as \(R_1 = 1\), \(R_2 = 2^{-2}\), \(R_3 = 2^{-4}\), and \(R_4 = 2^{-5}\), with respect to the different scaling of the spatial dimension. and \(d_{k_i}\) is the dimension of the key vectors. The function \(\text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}_i, \mathbf{V}_i)\) represents the concatenation of multiple attention heads. 

2. \textbf{Feed-Forward Network (FFN):} The FFN is applied per feature token to enhance feature-specific time series representation. It leverages the Universal Approximation Theorem to uncover complex patterns within the time series data:
\begin{equation}
\text{FFN}(\mathbf{Z}) = \delta(\mathbf{Z} \mathbf{W}^1 + \mathbf{b}^1)
\end{equation}
where \(\delta\) is the ReLU activation function, and \(\mathbf{W}^1 \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}\) and \(\mathbf{b}^1 \in \mathbb{R}^{d}\) represent the network weight matrix and bias vector, respectively.

3. \textbf{Layer Normalization:} Layer normalization is applied to each feature's time series to mitigate non-stationarity and measurement inconsistencies, defined by:
\begin{equation}
\text{LayerNorm}(\mathbf{Z}) = \frac{\mathbf{Z} - \mu(\mathbf{Z})}{\sigma(\mathbf{Z})}
\end{equation}
with \(\mathbf{Z}\) representing the hidden state, \(\mu(\mathbf{Z})\) its mean, and \(\sigma(\mathbf{Z})\) its standard deviation.

\textbf{Projection Layer:} The final predictive output is obtained through a projection layer that translates the final hidden state into the predicted future series values:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{:, f} = \mathbf{W}_p \mathbf{Z}^{L}_f + \mathbf{b}_p
\end{equation}
Here, \(\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{:, f}\) represents the predicted future values for feature \( f \), and \(\mathbf{Z}^{L}_f\) is the final hidden state of the last layer \( L \) for feature \( f \). The projection weight matrix \(\mathbf{W}_p \in \mathbb{R}^{S \times d}\) and the bias vector \(\mathbf{b}_p \in \mathbb{R}^{S}\) are used to generate the predictions.


