\section*{Appendix}\label{sec:appendix}

After estimating the item parameters $\alpha_j$, $\beta_j$ and the school effects $s_k$, individual abilities can be recovered using the \textit{expected a posteriori} (EAP) method \citep[e.g.,][]{bock1981marginal,rabe2008multilevel}:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}(\theta_{ik} \mid \mathbf{x}_{ik})
&= \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \theta_{ik}\,p(\theta_{ik}\mid \mathbf{x}_{ik})\,d\theta_{ik} \\
&= \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \theta_{ik}\,\frac{p(\mathbf{x}_{ik}\mid \theta_{ik})\,g(\theta_{ik})}{p(\mathbf{x}_{ik})}\,d\theta_{ik} \\
&= \frac{\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \theta_{ik}\,p(\mathbf{x}_{ik}\mid \theta_{ik})\,g(\theta_{ik})\,d\theta_{ik}}{\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} p(\mathbf{x}_{ik}\mid \theta_{ik})\,g(\theta_{ik})\,d\theta_{ik}}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Applying Gaussian-Hermite quadrature, the above integrals are approximated by weighted sums at the quadrature points:

\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}(\theta_{ik} \mid \mathbf{x}_{ik})
\approx
\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{q} V(n)\,\Bigl[\prod_{j=1}^{J}\pi_{ijk}(n)^{x_{ijk}}\bigl(1-\pi_{ijk}(n)\bigr)^{1-x_{ijk}}\Bigr]\,A(n)}
{\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{q} \Bigl[\prod_{j=1}^{J}\pi_{ijk}(n)^{x_{ijk}}\bigl(1-\pi_{ijk}(n)\bigr)^{1-x_{ijk}}\Bigr]\,A(n)}.
\end{equation}

We collect all fixed parameters $\{\alpha_j, \beta_j, s_k \mid j=1,\dots,J, k=1,\dots,K\}$ into a single vector $\Gamma$, which has length $2J + K$ under the 2PL model. The standard errors of these estimates are derived from the Hessian matrix of the log-likelihood:
\begin{equation}
    H(\hat{\Gamma}) = \frac{\partial^2 \ell(\hat{\Gamma})}{\partial \hat{\Gamma}\,\partial \hat{\Gamma}^\mathsf{T}},
\end{equation}

and the corresponding variance-covariance matrix is
\begin{equation}
    \operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Gamma}) = -\,H(\hat{\Gamma})^{-1}.
\end{equation}
Finally, the standard error for each parameter can be obtained by taking the square root of the corresponding diagonal element of $\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\Gamma})$.
