\subsection*{Data Collection based on Wikipedia2vec}
\label{subsec:datacolle}
Ultimately, we aim to leverage large language models to automatically pinpoint rising technologies across a broad spectrum of research areas instead of concentrating on just one area or limiting ourselves to technologies that have only recently gained popularity. Current lists of emerging technologies ~\cite{gartner2024emerging30, wef2024top10, mckinsey2024techtrends} are often limited and lack depth and complex categorisation. We aspire to use insights from language models to simplify the exploration of emerging technologies and employ machine learning techniques to systematically group these technologies into categories. Moreover, we create a set of technology indices from reliable data sources to both validate and add robustness to our list of emerging technologies. These are designed to offer governments, entrepreneurs, and researchers valuable perspectives.

It has been observed that previous efforts have utilised the hyperlink structure of Wikipedia to identify more extensive lists of emerging technologies ~\cite{bonaccorsi2020emerging}. However, these approaches overlooked the textual content of Wikipedia and failed to recognise the structure of these technologies or create additional metrics for comparing them. While Wikipedia may be less traditionally authoritative than patents, publications, and news from academic datasets, one significant drawback is that the naming of emerging technologies can vary across different publications, depending on the authors’ preferences and interpretations ~\cite{jaeger2015impact}. This is a phenomenon particularly pronounced with emerging technologies. Wikipedia’s advantage lies in providing each technology with a consistent and universally recognised name and a detailed and standardised description. Wikipedia’s widespread use as a source text for major language models (like BERT and Llama) underscores its broad acceptance and utility.

To create the ET100 dataset, we start with an extensive list of technology-related Wikipedia articles based on their distance from a seed article using a multidimensional model of Wikipedia. The multidimensional model of Wikipedia is created using an open-source vectorisation toolkit known as Wikipedia2vec. 

Wikipedia2vec ~\cite{yamada2018wikipedia2vec} is a toolkit that generates embeddings from each article and word within Wikipedia. With this toolkit, Wikipedia articles are trained as entities and thus represent the concepts and linkages within a broad context of 2.6 Million other articles. The embeddings for the English language Wikipedia (2018) are used for this research. However, embeddings are also available in eleven other languages, and researchers can also train their own embeddings with the tool as well.

It is a powerful tool to leverage the vast amount of knowledge encoded in Wikipedia to create high-quality word embeddings. It uses a skip-gram model, a type of neural network architecture that learns to predict the context of a given word based on its embedding. It then applies some modifications to the original skip-gram model to incorporate the structure of Wikipedia. For example, it uses the categories, links, and redirects in Wikipedia to define the context of a given word. It also uses a negative sampling technique to train the model efficiently. Word embeddings represent words in a vector space, while entity embeddings do the same for Wikipedia entities, such as articles for people, places, and concepts. Since the embedding space captures semantic similarities based on Wikipedia's context, co-occurrence and linkage structure, we could access emerging technology-relevant articles based on the cosine similarity measurements. Wikipedia2vec includes embeddings for 2 million words and 2.6 million entities. In this research, the entity (Wikipedia article) "List of Emerging Technologies" is regarded as a seed to collect the most similar 100,000 words and entities with it. Cosine similarity is used to find similar Wikipedia articles in the embedding space generated by Wikipedia2Vec. It measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors, providing a metric for similarity independent of their magnitude. The whole list contains 45,149 words and 54,851 entities. Since entity embeddings offer the advantage of capturing entities' specific contexts and relationships more accurately than word embeddings, we only keep the entities as the universe of emerging technologies. 

To ensure alignment with other emerging technology frameworks, including those from the World Economic Forum, OECD, and MIT Technology Review, we incorporated an additional thirteen technologies found in Wikipedia that were not initially included in our set. These technologies are: ("Advanced Computer Techniques", "Biopharmaceutical", "Communication", "Computer security software", "Customer relationship management", "Educational technology", "Electronic data interchange", "Enterprise resource planning", "Financial technology", "Genetics", "Microsatellite", "Online service provider", "Research"). 

We name our new and complete entity list ET50k (54,864 entities), which includes technologies, theories, organisations, people and other concepts related to emerging technologies. In the next step of the analysis, we recognise that for our purposes, articles about people, organisations, and books may be "technology-adjacent" but not technologies themselves. These articles represent "noise" for our purposes, and we remove and minimise their impact at the next step using a range of Wikipedia metadata. Wikidata is an open, cooperative, and multilingual database of metadata about Wikipedia articles. It consists of structured information to support Wikipedia and Wikimedia Commons and arranges data into distinct items, each representing a concept or object marked by a unique identifier known as a property. Pageviews are another form of Wikipedia metadata that refers to the number of times a webpage has been visited by users. They serve as a critical metric for assessing the popularity of online content. We use Wikidata first, followed by pageviews to filter the ET50k.

