\subsection*{Discussion}
Our ET100 dataset manually identifies 100 emerging technologies with detailed insights, while the ET23k dataset encompasses a broader set of technologies, each richly characterised by multiple data-driven features. These datasets serve as comprehensive resources for stakeholders in technology and innovation, enabling detailed trend analysis, market research, and strategic planning across various sectors. 

The comparison between our emerging technology list and the OECD's dataset highlights distinct approaches to categorising and understanding technological advancements. While the OECD list offers a broad, policy-oriented overview suitable for strategic planning and international collaboration, our ET100 list provides a more granular and data-driven approach. The ET100 utilises advanced analytics, including Wikipedia2vec and hierarchical clustering, to offer real-time insights into technology trends. This method identifies critical technologies and provides detailed insights into their applications, making it especially useful for stakeholders needing up-to-date, actionable information.

We verify the four constructed indices to establish their accuracy and reliability in reflecting real-world trends and values. This ensures our dataset is a credible and robust technology assessment and forecasting tool. Significant positive correlations across these indices with real-world metrics like publication counts, patent filings, scholarly interest, and historical documentation validate their utility. For instance, the strong correlation between the Age Index and the First\_Pub\_Year underscores our method's accuracy in determining the technologies' inception year. Similarly, the correlations observed for the Awareness and Deeptech Indices with publication and patent data indicate that these indices effectively capture both public and academic engagement with technologies. These results support the robustness of the dataset's construction and demonstrate its applicability in tracking and analysing the evolution and impact of technologies in real-world scenarios.

