\section*{Usage Notes}

% The Usage Notes should contain brief instructions to assist other researchers with data reuse. This may include a discussion of software packages suitable for analysing the assay data files, suggested downstream processing steps (e.g. normalization, etc.), or tips for integrating or comparing the data records with other datasets. Authors are encouraged to provide code, programs or data-processing workflows if they may help others understand or use the data. Please see our code availability policy for advice on supplying custom code alongside Data Descriptor manuscripts.

% For studies involving privacy or safety controls on public access to the data, this section should describe in detail these controls, including how authors can apply them to access the data, what criteria will be used to determine who may access the data, and any limitations on data use. 

The Cosmos 1.0 dataset encompasses 23,544 technologies with a hierarchical structure, and 100 emerging technologies are manually verified. We enhance the dataset with unique technology indices—Age of Tech, Deeptech, Generality, and Tech Awareness—and enrich it with metadata from Wikipedia and data from third-party sources like Crunchbase, Google Books, and Google Scholar.

Academics and researchers can leverage this dataset to track technological advancements and trends within their fields of interest. Figure \ref{fig:pageviews} illustrates the monthly pageview counts movement of an example of custom lists of emerging technologies filtered by multiple technology indices. The technologies in the list show positive growth in public interest across the last three years. It easily filters out a highly customized technology list by different combinations of technology indices.

The structured clustering and indices facilitate a deeper understanding of how specific technologies evolve and their broader implications within the technology landscape. This can aid in curriculum development, informing educational content to include cutting-edge technology topics and fostering research that anticipates or responds to shifts in technological paradigms. In particular, this research can potentially increase the understanding of the antecedents and measurement of technology convergence. Most studies measuring technology convergence focus on the patent record~\cite{eilers2019patent, sun2024technology} or specific fields, such as robotics and internet-of-things~\cite{kose2019identifying, sun2024technology}. The ET23k provides a rich data source for analysing technology convergence on a broad scale, notably in focusing on the drivers, patterns, and anticipation of convergence~\cite{sick2022exploring}.

Policymakers can utilise the dataset to inform and support regional and national economic development strategies. The comprehensive data on technology evolution, including indices reflecting technologies' maturity and impact potential, can guide decisions on where to allocate resources, support emerging industries, and help leaders develop policies encouraging innovation and technology adoption.

The dataset offers investors and market analysts a valuable tool for identifying emerging technologies and assessing their potential impact and commercial viability. By understanding the position of technology within the broader landscape—its maturity, innovativeness, and relevance across different sectors—investors can make more informed decisions about where to direct funds. This can lead to better investment strategies, enhanced risk assessment, and a more robust technology portfolio.

Overall, the Cosmos 1.0 dataset is a critical resource that can deepen the understanding of the technology landscape, aiding various stakeholders in making informed decisions that align with technological trends and potential growth areas. This contributes to a more dynamic and responsive approach to education, policy-making, and investment in technology.


