\section{The impact of Galactic Populations of PBH}
\label{sec4.0}


We next examine the plausibility that a galactic population of PBH could play a role in shaping the orbits of exoplanets. Specifically, we wish to obtain an estimate of the number of close encounters throughout the galaxy, as well as the typical relative velocity of the flyby. Subsequently, in Section \ref{sec7} we will proceed to examine the resulting deviations to eccentricities and semi-major axes of exoplanets due to PBH encounters in a simplified setting. 

Since neither observations nor simulations currently give complete or accurate predictions for initial values, we will consider a simplified system in which the galaxy is entirely comprised of Jupiter-star systems (later we also consider Neptune-star systems). Thus, each star has a single planet whose initial eccentricity and semi-major axis are roughly Jupiter-like with $(e_0,a_0)\sim$  (0, 5 AU).  Our aim is to identify the late time values of the orbital parameters 
\beq
e_\infty &=e_0+\Delta e\\
a_\infty &=a_0+\Delta a.
\eeq
 These perturbations $\Delta a$ and $\Delta e$ receive contributions from standard astrophysical bodies, such as close encounters with stars, as well as potentially from encounters with hypothetical bodies such as PBH. Moreover, one expects that $\Delta a$ and $\Delta e$ will depend on the radial distance from the galactic centre, since the densities and velocities of stars and PBH will change depending on the star's location in the galaxy. 



