We initialize the environment with loads and generations at time step $t$, as well as initial outages from the contingency list. The state is collected and constructed. A random action is then applied to the environment, resulting in the generation of the next state and a corresponding reward. We use random actions to explore as many states as possible.
Then, we collect a dataset by recording system states and do-nothing actions until a sufficient number of state-action pairs are gathered. We use this dataset to train a neural network with the same architecture as the policy network that we will use in PPO. Note that we include an entropy regularization term in the training objective so that the learned do-nothing policy assigns a relatively high probability to the do-nothing action while maintaining non-negligible probabilities for other actions. This balance is crucial because the later reinforcement learning stage relies on adequate exploration to optimize the policy. 

Then, we pass on the do-nothing policy to the agent and improve it using PPO algorithm. During each step of the training process, the RL agent observes the current state and takes an action. This state is collected and constructed based on the environment. The action is then applied to the environment, resulting in the generation of the next state and a corresponding reward. The reward represents the environment's feedback on the agent's action, serving as a critical mechanism to guide the RL agent toward improved policies. Over time, the agent learns from its actions and the rewards associated with them.
