We first consider a simplified case, where DC power flow is used, and generations and loads are fixed. 

Power system states at time step $5$ is $\bm S_t = \{ S_1,...,S_n \} = \{l_1,...,l_8, l^o_1,...,l^o_8, l^e_1,...,l^e_8, \theta_1,...,\theta_5\}$, where $l_i$ is the status of the transmission line $i$, $l^o_8$ is the bus id to which the origin of the transmission line $i$ connects to, $l^e_i$ is bus id to which the extremity of the transmission line $i$ connects. 
%${P_^G_1, P^G_2, P_^L_1, P^L_2, P_^L_3}$ 
The operators operates the power system by changing the transmission line status, the buses that a line connects to based on the current state. These are represented actions $A_t$. The way of choosing an action is represented by the policy, which is $ \pi(a|s) = Pr(A_t=a, S_t = s)$. ($ \pi(a|s) $ can also represent a deterministic policy if it is preferred.)
The transition from $S_t$ to $S_{t+1}$ is determined by the power flow equations and the actions taken. 

When events occur and cause outages in the power system, operators must respond to maintain the system in a stable and security state by emergency control strategies. However, there are strategies to mitigate the cascading risks in the long run that can not be found using the traditional techniques and tools. The goal is to mitigate the long-term cascading risks. This goal can be represented by the cumulative reward. 
A reward function $R_t$ is defined at each time step $t$, which is used to evaluate the performance of a pocily. The reward is designed based on the objective of the operators. For example, it could be the margin of line flow to the capacity,  $R_t = 1/N \sum_i^{N_l} \left | \left ( P^{l_{imax}}_t - P^{l_i}_t \right )/P^{l_{imax}}_t \right |$, where $N_l$ it the total number of transmission lines. In our problem of mitigating the cascading, we let $R_t = -1$ if the cascade propagates; $R_t = -100$ if the system is down.   
The cumulative reward that considers the long term effect is $ G_t = \sum_{i=t+1}^T R_i$.
This definition of reward is more like a negative penalty, and our goal is to minimize the penalty, which is equivalent to maximize the negative penalty or reward in reinforcement learning. 

The objective is finding a policy that maximizes the expectation of cumulative reward $G_t$.  
\begin{align}
    \max_{\pi} E_{\pi}\left (G_t\right | S_t=s) = \max_{\pi} v_{\pi}\left( s \right)
\end{align}
where $T$ is the number of generations of a cascade, $v_{\pi}(s)$ is called the value function. Expectation is applied because a policy could be stochastic. Since a policy is conditional on the state, we express $G_t$ conditional on state $S$. 

Instead of directly searching for the best policy, it is usually convenient to find the optimal value function $v_{\pi}(s)$ that corresponding to a best policy. With the optimal value function, it is sufficient to find the best optimal actions. 
There are various methods of solving the deep reinforcement learning problem~\cite{suttonBook18}. 

\subsection{Action space}
Actions that an agent can take include: (1) change the status of a line; that is, connecting or disconnecting a line; (2) change the bus of a line, for example, change one end of line 1 from bus 1 to bus 2; (3) do nothing, we guess this is the optimal action in most cases. 
Why using this changing action instead of using setting action? If we use actions like setting the status of a line, when a line is connected, the action of setting the status of the line as connection is the same as do nothing; therefore, using a setting action would result in many actions that have the same effect to the power system. Using changing action would eliminate the redundancy actions and ensure actions would make an effect on the power system. 

The thinking is that reducing the action space, otherwise, a large action space with a great number of equivalent actions would increase the difficulty for RL learning process. 

Disconnected lines caused by initial outages are not allowed to be reconnected again. It is because initial outages are usually caused by external factors, such as lightning, which cannot be cleared in a short time. From the perspective of mathematical models, on the other hand, if disconnected lines in initial outages can be reconnected, the optimal option should always be reconnecting the lines in initial outages. 

In RL training, we should add some mechanism to avoid take actions on disconnected lines in initial outages. 

Considering the industry practice of system operators handling a limited number of corrective actions due to time, communication and coordination constraints, we limit the actions to one substation during each step. 
\subsection{Rewards}
If the cascade propagates, the reward is -1. If the agent is allowed to reconnect lines, the optimal solution could be reconnecting outaged lines. Moreover, if the agent is allowed to take many operations as possible, another optimal solution is disconnecting all lines. Both of the above solutions could terminate the cascading. 
However, if the agent is only allowed to operate one substation each step, the second solution could be prevented especially when the system is large, as it needs more steps to disconnect all lines. 

The agent's objective is to reduce cascading propagation. To do this, we need effective metrics to assess cascading impact and evaluate the agent's actions. For instance, disconnecting a generator is discouraged, and we impose a penalty on the reward for undesirable actions.  

