\subsection{Dynamic programming}
The state of the MDP is all line outages in a cascade (distinguish from [zhoups19], we use line outages in current states instead of only line outages in this generation), and we introduce a termination state to represent the stop of cascading by grouping the corresponding states. These states with new line outages and the termination state compromise the observation space. For instance, if a cascade is $\{\{0\}, \{2,4\}\}$, then the corresponding states are $s_{0} = \{0\}, s_{1} = \{0,2,4\}, s_{2} = \emptyset$.

Since the toy example is small, we can enumerate all possible states and obtain the dynamic of the cascading, thus the dynamic programming can be used to derive the theoretical optimal policy. DP decomposes the task into policy evaluation and policy improvement based on the Bellman equation and policy improvement theorem. 

