We begin by numerically verifying the theoretical results discussed in Section \ref{s:whySingVals}.  We implement SVD on $50\times n$ matrices containing Gaussian noise for $n=1$ to $5000$ and illustrate the singular value decay curve in Fig.~\ref{fig:SV_trend}. At low $n$ the maximum and minimum singular values are well apart from 1, which we call the ``fluctuation bounds." \textit{In this region, noise deviations could be misinterpreted as patterns (principal components) in data.} For a given matrix, the singular values between the bounds signify the apparent principal components rising from a finite sample size which should have been equal to one.



\subsection{Ensemble simulation analysis}
